"button" tabindex="0"><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-button docs-ml-promotion-no-button">NO THANKS</span></span></span><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-container"><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-button-wrapper docs-ml-promotion-yes-button-wrapper" role="button" tabindex="0"><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-button docs-ml-promotion-yes-button">USE THE APP</span></span></span></div></div></div></div><div id="docs-ml-header-id" class="docs-ml-header"><span class="docs-ml-header-item docs-ml-header-drive-link"><div id="docs-ml-header-drive-link-aria-label" style="display:none;">Go to Drive</div><a href="https://drive.google.com/u/2?usp=docs_web" aria-labelledby="docs-ml-header-drive-link-aria-label" role="button"><div class="docs-ml-header-icon-container"><div class="docs-ml-icon docs-ml-header-icon docs-ml-arrow-back-dir"><div class="docs-ml-img-container docs-ml-img docs-ml-arrow-back-icon"></div></div></div></a></span><span class="docs-ml-header-item docs-ml-header-document-title"><div class="docs-ml-header-document-title-text">Automated Video QA System Architecture</div></span><div id="docs-ml-header-edit-button-aria-label" style="display:none;">Edit</div><span class="docs-ml-header-item docs-ml-header-edit-button" aria-labelledby="docs-ml-header-edit-button-aria-label" role="button" tabindex="0"><div class="docs-ml-header-icon-container"><div class="docs-ml-icon docs-ml-header-icon"><div class="docs-ml-img-container docs-ml-img docs-ml-edit-icon"></div></div></div></span></div><div class="app-container"><div class="doc-container"><div class="doc"><div class="doc-content" style="background-color:#ffffff;max-width:468pt;padding:72pt 72pt 72pt 72pt"><h1 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:24pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:24pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Automated Video Verification and Surgical Repair Architecture: Deterministic Integrity Gates, Multimodal Inspection, and Segmented Remediation</span></h1><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Autonomous video production engines cannot rely on human quality control or unconstrained multimodal language models to verify rendered media. Entrusting quality verification to a monolithic vision model that evaluates an entire video file introduces unacceptable non-determinism, hallucinations of subjective defects, blind spots toward low-level container and bitstream corruptions, and high inference costs. Production media engineering requires an inverted verification pyramid: high-throughput deterministic analyzers at the foundation to validate bitstreams, container structures, and baseband audio/video signals; specialized computer vision and optical character recognition (OCR) models in the middle tier to enforce geometric safe zones and typographical accuracy; and lightweight, bounded multimodal models at the apex restricted strictly to semantic contract validation.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Deterministic verification must prove objective technical correctness before any cognitive model executes. Structural defects such as broken container atoms, corrupt Network Abstraction Layer (NAL) units, non-monotonic presentation time stamps (PTS), audio clipping, and silent dropouts are detectable with mathematical certainty in a single decoding pass. When defects occur, the pipeline must avoid full re-renders. By enforcing frame-accurate timeline segmentation during initial composition, the orchestration layer can isolate failures, generate structured machine-readable error reports with exact frame coordinates, invoke targeted worker tasks to re-render only the damaged sequence, and losslessly splice the repaired segment back into the container master.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">This specification establishes the architectural blueprint for the Automated Video QA, Verification, Publishability Gates, Failure Detection, and Surgical Repair Ecosystem. Governed by Hermes as the master director, this framework guarantees that every published artifact conforms to YouTube platform requirements, complies with broadcast-grade signal specifications, satisfies its underlying semantic script contract, and recovers autonomously from localized rendering anomalies.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">1. Executive Summary</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Autonomous publishing pipelines require an automated verification framework capable of providing mathematical proof of media integrity while executing bounded remediation when errors occur. Full-pipeline re-renders represent an unacceptable allocation of compute, introduce new stochastic rendering variables, and compound latency in autonomous publishing schedules. Consequently, the verification engine is decoupled into an analytical hierarchy that validates artifacts through progressive filtering stages.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The lowest tier executes deterministic container and bitstream checks using FFprobe and FFmpeg null decoders equipped with aggressive error-trapping flags. Any container truncation, missing elementary stream, or invalid bitstream packet triggers an immediate abort. The second tier evaluates continuous baseband signals: baseband silence, black frames, frozen frames, digital clipping, and loudness non-compliance (conforming to ITU-R BS.1770-4 and EBU R128) are captured through deterministic filtergraphs. The third tier isolates rendered text and captions, utilizing high-performance OCR pipelines (PaddleOCR) and forced-alignment phoneme models (WhisperX) to detect safe-area clipping, character corruption, and temporal caption drift against the master transcript. The final analytical tier applies bounded Vision-Language Models (VLMs) and embedding cross-correlations (SigLIP) to discrete, sampled keyframes to evaluate semantic contract fulfillmentâ€”specifically verifying subject presence, visual-script coherence, and scene context.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Every analytical check emits a standardized, machine-readable finding referencing exact timecodes, frame indices, confidence scores, and repairability classifications. If a check crosses a blocking threshold, the publishability gate flags the artifact, halts downstream distribution, and compiles a targeted Repair Intent. The orchestrator dispatches this intent to the original rendering worker (such as Remotion), which re-renders only the bounded frame window via frame-range parameters. The resulting patch is verified in isolation and spliced into the master container using the FFmpeg concat demuxer via stream copying. Infinite regression is prevented through stateful failure fingerprinting, bounded retry budgets, and deterministic escalation paths back to the Hermes director.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">2. QA Capability Matrix</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The following matrix categorizes all verification capabilities required within the autonomous production engine, classifying their architectural execution mode, candidate tooling, local execution status, and automation maturity.</span></p><table style="border-spacing:0;border-collapse:collapse;margin-right:auto"><thead><tr style="height:0pt"><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1